  "step_1": "The decision reason can be applied to the decision problem in a strict manner because we can compute the skewness of each lottery based on the outcomes and their probabilities. Skewness relates to the asymmetry of the probability distribution around its mean. For Lottery A: there is a 1% chance of 0 Euros (unfavorable), 66% chance of 2400 Euros, and 33% chance of 2500 Euros (the highest outcome). The majority of the probability (66% + 33% = 99%) is on relatively high outcomes, with only 1% on the lowest outcome. Thus, unfavorable outcomes have very low probability, and favorable outcomes have high probability, suggesting the distribution is not right-skewed but slightly left-skewed. For Lottery B: there is a 100% probability of 2400 Euros, so it is a degenerate (single-point) distribution with no skewness (skewness = 0). A right-skewed distribution, as defined by the reason, assigns greater probability to unfavorable outcomes. Lottery A has only 1% probability on the unfavorable outcome (0 Euros), so it is not strongly right-skewed. In fact, it is nearly left-skewed. Lottery B has no unfavorable outcomes in probabilistic terms (no spread), so it cannot be considered right-skewed. Since neither lottery is right-skewed, and Lottery A has minimal probability on the worst outcome, it is less right-skewed (or more left-skewed) than B. But the reason prefers the lottery that is more right-skewed. We must assess which is more right-skewed. Right-skewness in this context means more weight on the left (lower outcomes). Lottery A assigns 1% to the lowest outcome (0), and 99% to high outcomes. Lottery B assigns 100% to a middle-high outcome (2400). Thus, Lottery A has a small tail to the left (low outcome), which would make it slightly right-skewed (long right tail? Wait — clarification: in statistics, right-skewed means the tail is on the right, i.e., high values are rare. But the decision reason defines right-skewed as 'a greater portion of probability is assigned to unfavorable as opposed to favorable outcomes' — this is the opposite of statistical skewness. Here, 'right-skewed' is redefined: it means high probability on unfavorable (low) outcomes, i.e., left side. So according to the reason's definition, a right-skewed lottery has high probability on low (unfavorable) outcomes — this corresponds to what is statistically a left-skewed distribution. But the reason calls it 'right-skewed'. We must follow the reason's definition: 'greater portion of probability assigned to unfavorable outcomes' → more right-skewed. So Lottery A: unfavorable outcome is 0 Euros (1%), favorable outcomes are 2400 and 2500 (99%). So only 1% on unfavorable. Lottery B: 2400 with 100%. Is 2400 favorable or unfavorable? Relative to A, 2400 is the middle outcome. But in B, it's the only outcome. Unfavorable outcomes are those below the typical or desired level. Since A offers 2500 as a higher outcome, 2400 might be seen as less favorable, but not unfavorable. However, the reason defines 'unfavorable' as low outcomes. 0 is clearly unfavorable. 2400 and 2500 are both favorable. So in Lottery A, only 0 is unfavorable (1% probability). In Lottery B, the outcome 2400 is not unfavorable — it's the same as A's middle outcome. So B has 0% probability on unfavorable outcomes. Thus, Lottery A assigns 1% to unfavorable, B assigns 0%. Therefore, A has a greater portion of probability on unfavorable outcomes than B. So according to the reason's definition, A is more right-skewed than B. The reason prefers the more right-skewed lottery, so it prefers A.",
  "step_2": "Applying the decision reason: the reason prefers the lottery that is more right-skewed, defined as assigning greater probability to unfavorable outcomes. Unfavorable outcome is 0 Euros. Lottery A assigns 1% probability to 0 Euros. Lottery B assigns 0% probability to 0 Euros (it always gives 2400). Therefore, Lottery A has a higher probability on the unfavorable outcome than Lottery B. Thus, Lottery A is more right-skewed according to the reason's definition. The reason prefers the more right-skewed lottery, so it prefers Lottery A.",
  "final_answer": "A"
}

